=== 13 HEXEYE - read the bytes yourself ===

file: examples/data/sample_win.exe  (3162624 bytes)

-- the first 64 bytes, as any hex editor would show them --
0x00000000  4D 5A 90 00 03 00 00 00  04 00 00 00 FF FF 00 00  |MZ..............|
0x00000010  8B 00 00 00 00 00 00 00  40 00 00 00 00 00 00 00  |........@.......|
0x00000020  00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00  00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00  |................|
0x00000030  00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00  00 00 00 00 80 00 00 00  |................|

   Look at the very first two bytes: 4D 5A. In the ASCII column: 'MZ'.
   Those are the initials of Mark Zbikowski, who designed the DOS
   executable format in 1983. Every Windows program still starts with
   his initials. File formats are archaeology.

-- walking the header by hand --
0x00  signature            : 4D 5A  ('MZ')
0x3C  e_lfanew             : 128  (0x00000080)  <- a pointer

      So: go to that offset and look.

0x00000080  50 45 00 00 64 86 0F 00  00 00 00 00 00 1E 2E 00  |PE..d...........|
0x00000090  89 0C 00 00 F0 00 22 00  0B 02 03 00 00 F4 0D 00  |......".........|

0x00000080  PE signature         : 'PE..'
  +04  Machine              : 0x8664  x64 (AMD64)
  +06  NumberOfSections     : 15
  +14  SizeOfOptionalHeader : 240
  +16  Characteristics      : 0x0022
  +08  TimeDateStamp        : 0  = 1970-01-01 00:00:00 UTC

      That is the COMPILE TIME, sitting in the file, in the clear.
      Compare it against when the file appeared on the victim's disk:
        * built long before it arrived  -> an off-the-shelf tool
        * built hours before it arrived -> compiled for this target
        * built in the future, or 1970, or all zeroes -> somebody
          scrubbed it, which is itself a finding

