                    search that hash on VirusTotal and you get the
                    family, not the sample.
   bin_entropy   -> {bytes: 3162624, entropy: 6.895605, path: examples/data/sample_win.exe}
   bin_is_go     -> true
   go_symbols    -> 21 user functions recovered
                    ...from a STRIPPED binary. Go's runtime needs
                    the symbol table for stack traces, so it cannot
                    be removed. Strip it and it is still there.

-- carving examples/data/memory_dump.bin --
   pe: 1 signature(s)
      first at 0x00000000:
0x00000000  4D 5A 00 01 4D 65 6D 6F  72 79 2D 53 6E 61 70 73  |MZ..Memory-Snaps|
0x00000010  68 6F 74 00 4E 4F 50 4E  4F 50 00 1F 00 00 00 39  |hot.NOPNOP.....9|

   fs_carve reports OFFSETS, not extracted files -- it says where a
   signature is, not how long the thing is, because the length lives
   inside the format and every format keeps it somewhere else.
   Extracting is your job, and you now have the tool for it:
   bytes_slice from the offset, and the format's own length field.

What you just did, in order:
  read a byte -> read a pointer -> follow it -> read a table
There is no step in any forensic tool that is harder than that.
There are only more offsets.
